/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  layoutDict -- the PLOT PLAN of the synthesis plant (DEV.md 4c C76).  A
  STARTING POINT for the groups, to copy into their own case and change.

  HOW THIS FILE WAS MADE.  `bin/choupo-layout --draft` wrote a proposal: the
  nine items whose sizers publish a diameter and a length in flow order on
  one line, and the seven others (the four compressors, the FEHE and the two
  ammonia tanks) listed for the author to place.  It was then arranged by
  hand into four rows, west to east in flow order:
    * the COMPRESSOR ROW to the south (y = -20 m), with the nitrogen,
      intercooler and aftercooler shells on the row north of it (y = -10 m);
    * the SYNTHESIS LOOP (y = +7 to +15 m): the FEHE under the converter,
      the water cooler and the chiller, the separator, the let-down drum and
      the storage flash;
    * the TANK FARM well to the south-east, downwind;
    * the HYDROGEN BUFFER's ten vessels (added with DEV.md 4c C89) to the
      south-west of the make-up train, two rows of five;
    * the CONTROL ROOM to the north-west, upwind, and an ELECTRICAL
      SUBSTATION west of the compressors -- both NON-PROCESS items;
    * a BUND round the tank farm and a kerbed slab under the loop's liquid
      vessels draining to a remote pit (DEV.md 4c C134, `bunds` below).
  Re-draw from scratch with `bin/choupo-layout --draft --force .`: it keeps
  the separation table, the wind, the declared boxes, the non-process items
  and the zones below, and lines the process items up again.

  WHAT IS THE ENGINE'S AND WHAT IS OURS.  The nine sized cylinders and the
  ten buffer vessels take their diameters and lengths from their own design
  sheets (design/<unit>/).
  EVERYTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE IS AN AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION FOR THE STUDENTS
  TO REPLACE WITH THEIR OWN SITE DATA: every position, every declared box,
  every separation distance, the wind, and every hazardous-area zone extent.
  They are round numbers chosen so that the checks are visible on this
  plant, not values from any spacing or hazardous-area standard.  Choupo
  ships no such table: the published ones (CCPS, GAP 2.5.2, Mecklenburgh;
  IEC 60079-10-1, EI 15, API RP 505) grant no redistribution, and a
  citation invented for a round number would turn "unsourced" into
  "falsely sourced".  A real project takes its distances and zone extents
  from the standard its client specifies, and its wind from the site's
  measured wind rose.

  THE REFERENCE PLANT RAISES NO FINDING (2026-11-09).  Until then this file
  left two on purpose for the groups to resolve: the two ammonia tanks 12 m
  apart against the 17 m declared between two tanks, and the electrical
  substation 8 m from the axis of MakeupComp1's zone 2 (radius 9 m), an
  ignition source inside a hazardous area.  This is the plant shown as the
  course's reference, so both are resolved here, each the way a layout
  answers it:
    1. the tanks stand 18 m apart, shell to shell (tank2 moved east inside
       the same bund), 1 m more than the declared 17 m;
    2. the substation stands west of the compressor row, upwind of every
       release source and outside every zone, and the separation table now
       says which distance it needs (the substation rows below).
  The checks still fire on any copy that breaks them: move tank2 back to
  x 181 m, or the substation back to (16, -31) m, and the run ANNOUNCES the
  shortfall and the ignition source in the zone (console, the caveat block,
  the result JSON and Reports -> "Plant layout (3D)") rather than refusing:
  a short distance is a layout a student must see and defend, while an
  overlap is not a layout at all and is refused.

  THE SITE (2026-11-09, the method of ammonia02_full_plant, DEV.md 4c C90,
  C93).  A ring road round the process block (main, east, north and west
  roads), the main road carried west past the hydrogen buffer, a tank-farm
  road along the bund's north side, an ENTRANCE road north to the MAIN GATE
  by the control room (upwind: the wind blows from the north-west), and an
  EMERGENCY GATE at the east end of the tank-farm road, so the fire brigade
  has a second way in that reaches the tanks first.  The FENCE is the plot
  boundary; every pipe that enters or leaves the plant crosses it at a
  labelled point.  Drawn since 2026-11-10 (DEV.md 4c C268): the UTILITIES BLOCK -- cooling
  tower, refrigeration package and flare, in the north-east corner, each
  a non-process box sized on a number the run publishes (see `items`).
  Drawn since 2026-11-13 (DEV.md 4c C268, slice 2): the UTILITY LINES
  (`utilityLines` at the end of this file).  A utility is not a flowsheet
  stream, so the engine routes nothing for one by itself; this file DECLARES
  each line, from an item to an item with a service word, and the engine
  routes it on the pipe rack, on the tiers above the process pipes', and
  checks it like a process pipe.  Declared here: cooling water from the
  cooling tower to each of the four coolers and back (eight lines), the
  refrigerant from the refrigeration package to the Chiller and back (two),
  and a flare header from each of the three loop vessels that vent (the
  separator and the two let-down drums) to the flare (three).  EVERY LINE IS
  UNSIZED, and says so: the layout sizes no pipe, process or utility.  The
  cooling-water and refrigerant lines carry the flow the run's own utility
  allocation publishes for the exchanger they serve; the flare lines carry
  none, because the case declares no relief valve and its three vent gases
  are already piped to the fence as process streams -- what a line to the
  flare would carry has no number in this run.
  Not drawn, said rather than invented: a steam line (the run draws 0.04
  kg/s of low-pressure steam for the separator and the let-down drum, 82 kW
  in all, from a steam system outside this battery limit that has no item on
  this plot to start a line from), the electrolysers, the air separation
  unit and the cooling-water make-up and blowdown -- all outside this battery
  limit, and named as such where their streams cross the fence.

  THE STORES ARE ON THEIR STREAMS (2026-11-09, DEV.md 4c C268).  The ten
  hydrogen-buffer vessels and the two ammonia tanks are stores ATTACHED to a
  boundary stream in system/postDict (DEV.md 4c C44, C89), not flowsheet
  units; their sizers wrote the stream into every vessel's sheet, and the
  engine routes that stream THROUGH the store: H2Feed from the west fence
  down to a HEADER between the two rows of buffer vessels (a main in the
  aisle at y = -50 m, one branch to each vessel's low nozzle), then back
  up to the rack and on to the make-up mixer; ProductNH3 from the storage
  flash along the rack to a main 1 m north of the two tanks (inside the
  bund), a branch to each tank, and from the main's east end to the east
  fence, the loading point.  Nothing in this file names the stream a store
  sits on: the sizing entry already does, and a second home would be the
  arity sin.  Each branch is a pipe of its own in the register
  (`pipe:H2Feed@H2Buffer/vessel1`, ...), with the stream's state and no
  flow: a store fills and empties by it, and no number for that is
  computed.  The branches fill and empty each item by ONE connection; the
  separate rundown and loading lines, pumps and loading arms of a real tank
  farm are not modelled, and the run says so on every header.

  Grammar and checks: docs/design/a-plant-layout-is-declared-and-checked.md
