//  (system/postDict, `economics { land { ... } }`).
plot
{
    margin  5 m;
    reason  "author-set assumption for this exercise: the fence and a 5 m clear strip inside it; the roads are declared in `roads` and lie inside the envelope; not taken from any spacing standard";
}

//  THE FENCE (2026-11-09; DEV.md 4c C93).  The engine draws it from the PLOT
//  above -- the plot's boundary, moved `inset` inward -- so its line is typed
//  nowhere.  It opens at each gate, as wide as the road that serves it.
//  Every pipe that enters or leaves the plant crosses it at a published
//  point, and each crossing says where the stream comes from or goes to.
//  The two feeds arrive from plants OUTSIDE this battery limit (the project
//  base data's electrolysers and air separation unit); the three gas outlets
//  leave on the north side, near the units that make them, to treatments
//  this case does not model (its README: the purge scrubber and the
//  let-down gas recovery are the groups' work).  Every origin and
//  destination is an AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION for this exercise, not a survey.
fence
{
    source  "author-set assumption for this exercise; not taken from any site survey or fencing standard";
    reason  "the site boundary: the land the plot prices, closed by a fence with two gates, and the point where every pipeline enters or leaves the plant";
    inset   0 m;
    crossings
    (
        { stream H2Feed;     from "the electrolyser plant, outside this battery limit: hydrogen at 30 bar (the pipe runs through the H2Buffer header, a store attached to this stream, before the make-up mixer)";  side west; }
        { stream N2Feed;     from "the air separation unit, outside this battery limit: nitrogen at 8 bar";  side west; }
        { stream Purge;      to   "purge-gas treatment outside this battery limit (the scrubber and hydrogen recovery are not modelled)";  side north;  at 55 m; }
        { stream LetdownGas; to   "let-down gas recovery outside this battery limit (not modelled)";  side north;  at 70 m; }
        { stream FlashGas;   to   "storage flash-gas recovery outside this battery limit (not modelled)";  side north;  at 78 m; }
        { stream ProductNH3; to   "the ammonia product: the pipe runs through the NH3Storage header (the two refrigerated tanks, a store attached to this stream) to this crossing, the loading point; shipped from outside this battery limit";  side east; }
    );
}

//  SECONDARY CONTAINMENT (DEV.md 4c C134).  Every item in the open that holds
//  liquid by the run's own inventory (system/postDict) must have a
//  containment round it, or the run ANNOUNCES it.  Here there are two:
//
//    * tankFarm -- a BUND round the two refrigerated ammonia tanks.  A spill
//      is held where it falls; a high wall keeps the cold pool small and
//      close (less evaporation, a smaller cloud), at the cost of access.
//      Rule: 110 % of the LARGEST tank's volume (the largest tank fails, not
//      both), plus 100 mm of rain over the bund's floor.  The engine takes
//      off the volume the tanks occupy below the wall's top -- here the
//      DECLARED 34 m boxes, which overstate a 34 m round tank's 908 m2 of
//      floor: an honest declaration costs wall height.  With a 4 m wall the
//      bund holds 20 672 m3 against the 20 435 m3 its rule requires.  A
//      lower wall needs a much larger floor (try 2 m: the run announces the
//      shortfall), and a FULL-CONTAINMENT tank, whose outer wall is the bund,
//      is the answer most large refrigerated stores take -- the groups'
//      call.
//    * loopKerb -- the loop's three liquid vessels (the separator, the
//      let-down drum and the storage flash, whose liquid holdups the postDict
//      declares) on a kerbed slab that drains to a REMOTE pit north of the
//      loop: an IMPOUNDING area, so a leak runs away from the vessels.  Rule:
//      100 % of the largest liquid holdup plus 50 mm of rain over the slab
//      and the pit.
//
//  The 110 % and 100 % margins and the rainfall depths are AUTHOR-SET
//  ASSUMPTIONS for this exercise.  They are the case's reading of the usual
//  form of a bund rule, NOT a transcription of any code: the margin, the
//  allowances and the design rainfall belong to the code the project's
//  client specifies, read and cited by the groups.  Choupo ships no such
//  table (the published ones grant no redistribution).
bunds
(
    {
        name        "tankFarm";
        kind        bund;
        items       ( "NH3Storage/tank1" "NH3Storage/tank2" );
        region      { xMin 103 m; xMax 213 m; yMin -69 m; yMax -1 m; }
        wall        { height 4 m; }
        drain       closedValve;
        capacityRule
        {
            basis   vesselVolume;
            terms   ( { fraction 1.1; of largest; } );
            allowances
            (
                { name rain; depth 100 mm; reason "a design storm falling on the bund floor, as a round author-set number; the site's own rainfall statistics replace it"; }
            );
            source  "author-set assumption for this exercise: the case's reading of the usual form of a bund rule (a margin over the largest tank, plus rain); not a transcription of any code";
            reason  "the largest tank fails, not both; the margin covers surge and foam, the allowance the rain that collects in the bund";
        }
        source      "author-set assumption for this exercise; not a civil design";
        reason      "the two refrigerated ammonia tanks share one bund: compatible contents, one drain kept closed and opened by hand only once the water is seen to be clean";
    }
    {
        name        "loopKerb";
        kind        impounding;
        items       ( "Separator" "LetdownHP" "StorageFlash" );
        region      { xMin 58 m; xMax 82 m; yMin 10 m; yMax 18 m; }
        wall        { height 0.2 m; }
        pit         { x 70 m; y 24 m; length 4 m; width 3 m; depth 1.5 m; }
        drain       sumpPump;
        capacityRule
        {
            basis   heldLiquid;
            terms   ( { fraction 1; of largest; } );
            allowances
            (
                { name rain; depth 50 mm; reason "a design storm on the slab and the open pit, as a round author-set number"; }
            );
            source  "author-set assumption for this exercise: the case's reading of a kerbed process area draining to a remote pit; not a transcription of any code";
            reason  "a leak from the loop's liquid ammonia vessels is led away from them to the pit, which holds the largest holdup";
        }
        source      "author-set assumption for this exercise; not a civil design";
        reason      "the loop's three liquid vessels stand on one kerbed slab, sloped to a pit 6 m north of it, outside the slab";
    }
);

//  THE UTILITY LINES (2026-11-13; DEV.md 4c C268, slice 2).  Every line is
//  DECLARED: a name, a service word, the item the flow leaves and the item it
//  reaches (both placed above), and the case's reason.  The engine routes it
//  on the pipe rack, on tiers ABOVE the process pipes' (the tower's and the
//  chiller's lines on their own tier each, a supply beside its return), lands
//  each end on a port of its item (the item's top unless a face is declared:
//  the flare's lines enter its base, on the west face 2 m up), and checks it
//  as it checks a process pipe -- through an item, over a road below the
//  headroom, through a bund wall.  NO LINE IS SIZED (the layout sizes no pipe).
//  `carries` names the unit and the utility whose allocation row of the run
//  is the flow the line carries; the engine reads that row and refuses a
//  name the run does not carry.  The flare lines carry nothing (see the header).
